(window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS = window.PSAC_DEMO_DECKS || {})[3] = {"grade":3,"packs":{"grade3-english":{"g3eng-listening":[{"id":"g3eng-lis-034","q":"Read: \"The egg hatched. Then a tiny chick came out. Next, the chick grew feathers. Finally, it learned to fly.\" What happened IMMEDIATELY AFTER the egg hatched?","opts":["A tiny chick came out","The egg was laid","The chick grew feathers","The chick learned to fly"],"a":"A tiny chick came out","hint":"\"Immediately after\" means the very next step.","exp":"<b>A tiny chick came out</b> is correct. The word \"Then\" tells us that the chick coming out was the next event after hatching."},{"id":"g3eng-lis-060","q":"A recipe says: \"Step 1: Peel the potatoes. Step 2: Boil them for 20 minutes.\" What is the purpose of this text?","opts":["To give instructions for cooking potatoes","To tell a funny story about a busy chef","To persuade you to eat far more potatoes","To describe what fresh potatoes look like"],"a":"To give instructions for cooking potatoes","hint":"Numbered steps are always instructions.","exp":"A recipe uses numbered steps to tell you exactly what to do and in what order, so its purpose is <b>to give instructions</b>."},{"id":"g3eng-lis-047","q":"Read: \"The baby crawled, then walked, then ran.\" What did the baby do BEFORE running?","opts":["Crawled","Skipped","Jumped","Walked"],"a":"Walked","hint":"What comes right before \"ran\"?","exp":"<b>Walked</b> came before running. The order is: crawled → walked → ran."},{"id":"g3eng-lis-005","q":"Read: \"Reading books is a great habit. Books teach us new words and ideas. They also help us imagine wonderful worlds.\" What is the passage mainly about?","opts":["Why reading books is good","How to buy books at a shop","Writing your own long story","How the town library works"],"a":"Why reading books is good","hint":"All the sentences talk about something good that books do.","exp":"Every sentence gives a benefit of reading, so the passage is about <b>why reading books is good</b>. It never mentions shops, writing or libraries."},{"id":"g3eng-lis-032","q":"Which word tells you what happened FIRST in a story?","opts":["Finally","First","After","Then"],"a":"First","hint":"Only one of these words signals the very beginning.","exp":"<b>First</b> signals the beginning of a sequence. \"Then\" and \"After\" come in the middle; \"Finally\" comes at the end."}],"g3eng-speaking":[{"id":"g3eng-spk-052","q":"Which question word asks about a PLACE?","opts":["What","Where","Who","When"],"a":"Where","hint":"This word asks about location.","exp":"<b>Where</b> asks about a place or location. e.g. \"Where is the library?\" — the answer will name a place."},{"id":"g3eng-spk-061","q":"Which question would you ask to find out WHAT something is?","opts":["Who is that animal?","What is that animal?","When is that animal?","Where is that animal?"],"a":"What is that animal?","hint":"\"What\" asks for information about a thing.","exp":"<b>What is that animal?</b> uses \"what\" to ask for the name or identity of a thing."},{"id":"g3eng-spk-006","q":"Which word is the BEST description of a lemon?","opts":["sour","sweet","salty","bitter-hot"],"a":"sour","hint":"What do you feel on your tongue when you eat a lemon?","exp":"<b>Sour</b> is the correct word. Lemons have a tart, sour taste that makes your face scrunch up."},{"id":"g3eng-spk-059","q":"The answer is \"At the market.\" What question word started the question?","opts":["How","Where","When","Who"],"a":"Where","hint":"\"At the market\" names a PLACE.","exp":"<b>Where</b> asks about a place. \"At the market\" answers \"Where did you buy the fruit?\""},{"id":"g3eng-spk-033","q":"A pupil says: \"The dog. It barked. It ran.\" How could this be IMPROVED?","opts":["Dog. Barked. Ran. It went away fast.","It barked. It ran. It barked. It ran.","The large brown dog barked and ran away.","The dog. It barked loudly. It ran off."],"a":"The large brown dog barked and ran away.","hint":"Add adjectives and adverbs to give more detail.","exp":"<b>\"The large brown dog barked and ran away.\"</b> joins the fragments into one flowing sentence and adds adjectives (large, brown). The others are still a string of short, choppy pieces."}],"g3eng-reading":[{"id":"g3eng-rdr-006","q":"Read: \"The mango tree in our garden is very old. Grandpa planted it fifty years ago. Every summer it gives us plenty of sweet mangoes.\" Who planted the mango tree?","opts":["The writer","Grandma","A neighbour","Grandpa"],"a":"Grandpa","hint":"The passage tells you exactly who planted it.","exp":"<b>Grandpa</b> — the passage clearly states \"Grandpa planted it fifty years ago.\""},{"id":"g3eng-rdr-055","q":"Why are some words printed in BOLD in a non-fiction text?","opts":["Because the author made a mistake","Because they are the longest words","Because they are important key words","Because they are the first words used"],"a":"Because they are important key words","hint":"Bold text draws your eye — it is used to highlight something.","exp":"<b>Bold</b> is used to emphasise key words and important ideas that the reader should notice."},{"id":"g3eng-rdr-051","q":"What is the PURPOSE of a TITLE in a text?","opts":["To tell the reader what the text is about","To tell the reader who wrote the text","To list what is inside the text","To give extra information about a picture"],"a":"To tell the reader what the text is about","hint":"The title is the name of the text — what does a name do?","exp":"<b>To tell the reader what the text is about</b> — a title introduces the topic of the text so the reader knows what to expect."},{"id":"g3eng-rdr-059","q":"In a recipe, the METHOD section uses NUMBERED STEPS (1, 2, 3...). Why?","opts":["To show the order things must be done","To list the ingredients that are needed","To give the name of the chef who wrote it","To show how long each step will take"],"a":"To show the order things must be done","hint":"In instructions, ORDER matters — what feature shows order?","exp":"<b>Numbered steps</b> make the sequence clear, so you know exactly what to do first, second and third."},{"id":"g3eng-rdr-070","q":"Which text feature helps you understand the meaning of a difficult word WITHOUT going to a dictionary?","opts":["The number given to that chapter","The contents page at the very front","A glossary at the back of the book","A caption printed under a picture"],"a":"A glossary at the back of the book","hint":"This is the feature specifically designed to explain vocabulary.","exp":"A <b>glossary</b> at the back of a non-fiction book defines the difficult or technical words used in the text."}]},"grade3-french":{"g3fr-comprehension-orale":[{"id":"g3fr-co-045","q":"Dans un texte, quel mot indique ce qui arrive en PREMIER ?","opts":["Ensuite","D'abord","Enfin","Parce que"],"a":"D'abord","hint":"Il y a un ordre : d'abord → ensuite/puis → enfin.","exp":"Le mot <b>d'abord</b> (ou \"premièrement\", \"pour commencer\") indique le premier événement."},{"id":"g3fr-co-005","q":"Quel mot contient le son /v/ ?","opts":["dort","vache","farine","pied"],"a":"vache","hint":"Le son /v/ est comme dans \"voiture\".","exp":"<b>Vache</b> commence par le son /v/. Ne confonds pas /v/ avec /f/ !"},{"id":"g3fr-co-009","q":"Combien de fois entend-on le son /s/ dans \"soleil et cerise\" ?","opts":["1","2","3","0"],"a":"2","hint":"Écoute bien : so-leil et ce-rise. Le \"c\" devant e/i fait aussi le son /s/.","exp":"On entend le son /s/ dans <b>s</b>oleil et dans <b>c</b>erise (le \"c\" devant \"e\" fait le son /s/)."},{"id":"g3fr-co-006","q":"Quel mot commence par le son /d/ ?","opts":["sol","tante","dent","marche"],"a":"dent","hint":"Le son /d/ est comme dans \"données\". Il ressemble au son /t/ mais est différent.","exp":"<b>Dent</b> commence par /d/. Le mot \"tante\" commence par /t/, un son proche mais distinct."},{"id":"g3fr-co-057","q":"\"Où habites-tu ?\" — C'est quel type de message ?","opts":["Une suggestion","Une question","Une information","Un ordre"],"a":"Une question","hint":"La phrase se termine par \"?\" et demande une réponse.","exp":"<b>\"Où habites-tu ?\"</b> est une <b>question</b>. Elle utilise le mot interrogatif \"où\"."}],"g3fr-expression-orale":[{"id":"g3fr-eo-075","q":"La réponse est \"parce que j'adore les animaux\". Quelle était la question ?","opts":["Où travailles-tu avec les animaux ?","Qui est ton docteur à l'hôpital ?","Quand commences-tu ton nouveau travail ?","Pourquoi veux-tu devenir vétérinaire ?"],"a":"Pourquoi veux-tu devenir vétérinaire ?","hint":"\"Parce que\" répond toujours à \"Pourquoi\".","exp":"\"<b>Pourquoi</b>\" demande la raison. \"Parce que j'adore les animaux\" répond à \"Pourquoi veux-tu devenir vétérinaire ?\""},{"id":"g3fr-eo-019","q":"Quel mot est un synonyme de \"gentil\" ?","opts":["froid","méchant","aimable","dur"],"a":"aimable","hint":"Un synonyme de gentil décrit quelqu'un de sympathique et doux.","exp":"<b>Aimable</b> est un synonyme de <b>gentil</b>. On peut aussi dire \"sympathique\" ou \"doux\"."},{"id":"g3fr-eo-030","q":"Quel mot peut remplacer \"ensuite\" dans un récit ?","opts":["parce que","enfin","puis","d'abord"],"a":"puis","hint":"\"Puis\" et \"ensuite\" indiquent tous les deux ce qui vient après.","exp":"<b>Puis</b> est un synonyme de <b>ensuite</b>. Les deux servent à introduire la suite des événements."},{"id":"g3fr-eo-009","q":"Quel adjectif est le contraire de \"long\" ?","opts":["large","haut","court","grand"],"a":"court","hint":"Si une corde n'est pas longue, elle est ___.","exp":"Le contraire de <b>long</b> est <b>court</b>. Ex : un chemin long / un chemin court."},{"id":"g3fr-eo-034","q":"Lis les phrases et trouve l'ordre correct : A – Enfin, il dort. B – D'abord, il se lave les dents. C – Ensuite, il met son pyjama.","opts":["A, B, C","B, A, C","C, A, B","B, C, A"],"a":"B, C, A","hint":"Utilise les mots d'abord (B), ensuite (C), enfin (A).","exp":"L'ordre correct est <b>B (d'abord), C (ensuite), A (enfin)</b> : se laver → pyjama → dormir."}],"g3fr-lecture":[{"id":"g3fr-lec-008","q":"Quel mot a 4 syllabes ?","opts":["or-di-na-teur","mai-son","soleil","chat"],"a":"or-di-na-teur","hint":"Compte les syllabes : or-di-na-teur = 4.","exp":"\"<b>Ordinateur</b>\" a 4 syllabes : or – di – na – teur."},{"id":"g3fr-lec-041","q":"Lis : \"Demain, c'est mon anniversaire. J'aurai 8 ans. Mes amis viennent pour une fête.\" Quel âge va avoir la personne qui parle ?","opts":["8 ans","10 ans","9 ans","7 ans"],"a":"8 ans","hint":"Le texte donne l'âge directement.","exp":"Le texte dit : \"J'aurai <b>8 ans</b>.\""},{"id":"g3fr-lec-010","q":"Combien de syllabes a \"pa-pi-llon\" ?","opts":["3","2","1","4"],"a":"3","hint":"Divise : pa-pi-llon.","exp":"\"<b>Papillon</b>\" a <b>3 syllabes</b> : pa – pi – llon."},{"id":"g3fr-lec-043","q":"Lis : \"La sorcière prépare une potion magique. Elle met des herbes, une plume et de l'eau de pluie dans son chaudron.\" Que prépare la sorcière ?","opts":["Une potion magique","Un gâteau d'anniversaire","Une soupe de légumes","Un remède contre le rhume"],"a":"Une potion magique","hint":"Lis la première phrase.","exp":"Le texte dit : \"La sorcière prépare <b>une potion magique</b>.\""},{"id":"g3fr-lec-002","q":"Combien de syllabes a le mot \"chat\" ?","opts":["3","2","4","1"],"a":"1","hint":"\"Chat\" ne contient qu'une seule voyelle prononcée.","exp":"\"<b>Chat</b>\" a <b>1 syllabe</b>. Les mots courts souvent n'ont qu'une syllabe : chat, chien, fleur, pain."}]},"grade3-health":{"g3he-hygiene":[{"id":"g3he-hyg-002","q":"Which part of the body protects us from germs and infection?","opts":["The bones","The hair","The nails","The skin"],"a":"The skin","hint":"Our skin is the body's first line of defence.","exp":"Our <b>skin</b> is the body's main barrier against germs. Clean, unbroken skin prevents bacteria and viruses from entering our body."},{"id":"g3he-hyg-018","q":"Why is it important to drink clean (safe) water?","opts":["Clean water weighs less","Dirty water is colder","Dirty water is cheaper","Dirty water carries germs"],"a":"Dirty water carries germs","hint":"Think about water-borne diseases.","exp":"<b>Contaminated water</b> carries bacteria, viruses and parasites causing diseases like diarrhoea, cholera and typhoid. Clean water is essential."},{"id":"g3he-hyg-008","q":"What causes tooth plaque?","opts":["A thin layer of water","A coating of toothpaste","A sticky film of bacteria","A layer of clean saliva"],"a":"A sticky film of bacteria","hint":"Plaque needs food (sugar) to form.","exp":"<b>Plaque</b> is a sticky film of bacteria that forms on teeth. These bacteria are fed by sugar in food, producing acid that causes tooth decay."},{"id":"g3he-hyg-040","q":"A child notices a red, itchy rash after using a new soap. What should they do?","opts":["Stop using it, tell an adult","Put toothpaste on the rash","Wash more with the same soap","Ignore it for a few days"],"a":"Stop using it, tell an adult","hint":"Rashes after using a new product suggest a reaction.","exp":"A red, itchy rash after using a new product suggests an <b>allergic reaction or skin sensitivity</b>. Stop using the product immediately and consult a pharmacist or doctor."},{"id":"g3he-hyg-045","q":"In Mauritius, what is the most important season to be extra careful about mosquito-borne diseases?","opts":["Only during cyclones","The cool, dry winter","Every season is the same","The hot, rainy summer"],"a":"The hot, rainy summer","hint":"Think about when mosquitoes breed most.","exp":"Dengue and other Aedes mosquito-borne diseases peak in <b>Mauritius's hot, rainy summer</b> (November–April) when standing water is abundant and temperatures favour mosquito breeding."}],"g3he-nutrition":[{"id":"g3he-nut-008","q":"Iron is a mineral that helps our body to:","opts":["Make red blood cells","Build strong bones","Cool the body down","Digest carbohydrates"],"a":"Make red blood cells","hint":"Think about blood and oxygen transport.","exp":"<b>Iron</b> is used to make haemoglobin in red blood cells, which carries oxygen from the lungs to all parts of the body. Sources include meat, beans and leafy greens."},{"id":"g3he-nut-003","q":"Which nutrient is most important for building and repairing our body?","opts":["Vitamin C","Fat","Protein","Carbohydrates"],"a":"Protein","hint":"This nutrient is the building block of muscles and tissues.","exp":"<b>Protein</b> is essential for growth, building muscles and repairing damaged tissues. Good sources include meat, fish, eggs, beans and dairy."},{"id":"g3he-nut-050","q":"What is the role of fats in the diet (in the right amount)?","opts":["They do nothing useful","They only add flavour","They only add weight","They store energy"],"a":"They store energy","hint":"Fats do more than just provide calories.","exp":"<b>Dietary fats</b> provide concentrated energy, help absorb vitamins A, D, E and K, insulate the body against temperature changes and cushion organs. Healthy fats are necessary."},{"id":"g3he-nut-013","q":"Which foods protect us from disease and keep us healthy (protective foods)?","opts":["Sweets and chocolate","Fruits and vegetables","White bread and butter","Chips and fried food"],"a":"Fruits and vegetables","hint":"These foods are rich in vitamins and minerals.","exp":"<b>Fruits and vegetables</b> are protective foods. Their vitamins and minerals boost our immune system, protecting us from illness and disease."},{"id":"g3he-nut-051","q":"Why is urine colour a useful indicator of hydration?","opts":["Clear urine shows thirst","Dark urine is healthier","Urine colour means nothing","Dark urine shows dehydration"],"a":"Dark urine shows dehydration","hint":"The kidneys concentrate urine when water intake is low.","exp":"<b>Urine colour</b> is a reliable hydration signal. Pale yellow = well-hydrated. Dark yellow/orange = dehydrated — the kidneys are conserving water by concentrating urine."}],"g3he-safety":[{"id":"g3he-saf-005","q":"Why are safety signs and warnings important?","opts":["They are only for adults","They are only in factories","They decorate the wall","They warn us of danger"],"a":"They warn us of danger","hint":"Safety signs give us important information.","exp":"Safety signs <b>alert us to dangers</b> like slippery floors, electrical hazards or restricted areas, allowing us to take precautions."},{"id":"g3he-saf-002","q":"Which of these behaviours PREVENTS accidents at school?","opts":["Walking in the corridors","Ignoring safety signs","Climbing on the furniture","Running as fast as we can"],"a":"Walking in the corridors","hint":"Safe behaviour prevents most school accidents.","exp":"<b>Walking, using equipment correctly and following safety rules</b> prevents most common school accidents like falls, collisions and cuts."},{"id":"g3he-saf-033","q":"Why should we not run near a swimming pool?","opts":["Running is too noisy","It makes the pool dirty","Running splashes the water","Wet floors are slippery"],"a":"Wet floors are slippery","hint":"Think about the surface condition near pools.","exp":"Pool surrounds are always wet. Running on wet surfaces causes falls. If you hit your head on the pool edge or the hard ground, the result can be <b>fatal</b>. Always walk near pools."},{"id":"g3he-saf-041","q":"During the Mauritian cyclone season, why is it especially dangerous to walk near rivers and drains?","opts":["They only smell bad","They can flood very fast","They stay calm in storms","They are deeper in winter"],"a":"They can flood very fast","hint":"Cyclones bring very heavy rain.","exp":"Cyclone rainfall is intense. Rivers and drains can <b>rise from ankle to head height in minutes</b>, creating powerful currents that sweep people and vehicles away. Stay well back during heavy rain."},{"id":"g3he-saf-056","q":"A DRSABC framework helps remember the steps of emergency response. What does the \"D\" stand for?","opts":["Danger","Doctor","Dial","Drink"],"a":"Danger","hint":"You cannot help if you become a casualty yourself.","exp":"<b>D = Danger:</b> check the environment is safe before approaching a casualty. A second victim (the rescuer) doubles the problem. Safety first is ALWAYS first in emergency response."}]},"grade3-maths":{"g3mth-numbers":[{"id":"g3mth-num-051","q":"Which is greater: 100 or 99?","opts":["99","They are equal","Cannot tell","100"],"a":"100","hint":"100 has 1 hundred; 99 has 0 hundreds.","exp":"<b>100</b> is greater than 99."},{"id":"g3mth-num-004","q":"What is 8 hundreds, 0 tens and 5 ones?","opts":["508","580","805","850"],"a":"805","hint":"8 hundreds = 800, 0 tens = 0, 5 ones = 5.","exp":"800 + 0 + 5 = <b>805</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-num-036","q":"Round 712 to the nearest 100.","opts":["710","700","800","720"],"a":"700","hint":"Tens digit is 1 — round down.","exp":"712 rounded to the nearest 100 is <b>700</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-num-005","q":"What is the value of the digit 9 in 391?","opts":["90","9","39","900"],"a":"90","hint":"9 is in the tens place.","exp":"In 391, the 9 is in the tens place, so its value is <b>90</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-num-011","q":"Write 500 + 60 + 0 as a number.","opts":["560","605","506","650"],"a":"560","hint":"There are 0 ones.","exp":"500 + 60 + 0 = <b>560</b>."}],"g3mth-addition":[{"id":"g3mth-add-013","q":"A class collected 48 cans on Monday and 56 cans on Tuesday. How many cans in total?","opts":["114","94","103","104"],"a":"104","hint":"48 + 56 = ?","exp":"48 + 56: Ones 8+6=14, carry 1. Tens 4+5+1=10. Answer: <b>104</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-add-015","q":"Priya read 46 pages on Saturday and 67 pages on Sunday. How many pages did she read altogether?","opts":["113","103","112","123"],"a":"113","hint":"46 + 67 = ?","exp":"46 + 67: Ones 6+7=13, carry 1. Tens 4+6+1=11. Answer: <b>113</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-add-019","q":"Raj had 78 marbles. He won 43 more. How many does he have altogether?","opts":["121","131","120","111"],"a":"121","hint":"78 + 43 = ?","exp":"78 + 43: Ones 8+3=11, carry 1. Tens 7+4+1=12. Answer: <b>121</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-add-009","q":"Rani had 57 stickers and Dev gave her 38 more. How many stickers does she have now?","opts":["95","94","85","105"],"a":"95","hint":"57 + 38 = ?","exp":"57 + 38: Ones 7+8=15, carry 1. Tens 5+3+1=9. Answer: <b>95</b>."}],"g3mth-subtraction":[{"id":"g3mth-sub-015","q":"Priya had 74 sweets. She ate 28. How many sweets are left?","opts":["56","46","45","36"],"a":"46","hint":"74 − 28 = ?","exp":"74 − 28: Ones 14−8=6, Tens 6−2=4. Answer: <b>46</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-sub-019","q":"Raj scored 80 points in a game but lost 36 points. What is his final score?","opts":["44","54","43","34"],"a":"44","hint":"80 − 36 = ?","exp":"80 − 36 = <b>44</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-sub-013","q":"Rani had Rs 90 and spent Rs 54. How much does she have left?","opts":["Rs 36","Rs 26","Rs 35","Rs 46"],"a":"Rs 36","hint":"90 − 54 = ?","exp":"90 − 54 = <b>36</b>."},{"id":"g3mth-sub-009","q":"Dev had 82 cards. He gave 37 to his friend. How many does he have left?","opts":["55","44","45","35"],"a":"45","hint":"82 − 37 = ?","exp":"82 − 37: Ones 12−7=5, Tens 7−3=4. Answer: <b>45</b>."}]},"grade3-ssee":{"g3ssee-environment":[{"id":"g3ssee-env-001","q":"Which sense do we use to HEAR a bird singing?","opts":["Hearing","Sight","Smell","Touch"],"a":"Hearing","hint":"Think about which body part you use to listen.","exp":"We use the sense of <b>hearing</b> (our ears) to detect sounds like a bird singing."},{"id":"g3ssee-env-003","q":"Which sense do we use to SMELL a flower?","opts":["Taste","Touch","Hearing","Smell"],"a":"Smell","hint":"Which body part do we use to detect smells?","exp":"We use the sense of <b>smell</b> (our nose) to detect pleasant and unpleasant smells."},{"id":"g3ssee-env-008","q":"Riya puts her hand in cold water. Which sense is she using?","opts":["Sight","Hearing","Taste","Touch"],"a":"Touch","hint":"","exp":"Riya feels the temperature of the water using the sense of <b>touch</b>."},{"id":"g3ssee-env-010","q":"Which sense organ do we use to hear thunder during a storm?","opts":["Eyes","Nose","Tongue","Ears"],"a":"Ears","hint":"","exp":"We use our <b>ears</b> to hear sounds, including loud thunder."},{"id":"g3ssee-env-025","q":"Ali describes his environment as having big trees, birds and rivers. What type of environment does Ali live near?","opts":["School environment","Market environment","Home environment","Natural environment"],"a":"Natural environment","hint":"","exp":"Big trees, birds and rivers are features of the <b>natural environment</b> — they occur naturally in nature."}],"g3ssee-family":[{"id":"g3ssee-fam-001","q":"Which of the following is a PERSONAL DETAIL that helps identify who you are?","opts":["The bus fare","The weather","The shop name","Your name"],"a":"Your name","hint":"Personal details are things that describe YOU.","exp":"Your <b>name</b> is a personal detail — it identifies you. Your age, address and school name are personal details too. The weather, a bus fare and a shop name describe the world, not you."},{"id":"g3ssee-fam-007","q":"Alisha is 8 years old and lives in Quatre Bornes. What is Quatre Bornes in relation to her?","opts":["Her school","Her first name","Her locality","Her age group"],"a":"Her locality","hint":"","exp":"Quatre Bornes is the town (<b>locality</b>) where Alisha lives — part of her home address. It is not her age, her name or her school."},{"id":"g3ssee-fam-008","q":"Which TWO of these are personal details?","opts":["Rice and roads","Name and age","Trees and sand","Rain and wind"],"a":"Name and age","hint":"","exp":"A person's <b>name</b> and <b>age</b> are personal details that identify them. Rice, roads, rain, wind, trees and sand are general things in the world, not details about a person."},{"id":"g3ssee-fam-004","q":"Which of the following is NOT a personal detail?","opts":["Your school name","The price of sugar","Your date of birth","Your home address"],"a":"The price of sugar","hint":"","exp":"The price of sugar is a <b>fact about a product</b>, not about a person. Personal details relate directly to an individual."},{"id":"g3ssee-fam-015","q":"Which of the following is a TALENT?","opts":["Having brown hair","Living in Curepipe","Playing the piano","Being very tall"],"a":"Playing the piano","hint":"A talent is a skill someone is good at.","exp":"Playing the piano is a <b>talent</b> — a special ability a person has learned and developed. Where you live, your hair colour and your height are not talents."}],"g3ssee-natural":[{"id":"g3ssee-nat-003","q":"What is the main function of the STEM of a plant?","opts":["To hold the plant up and carry water","To store the seeds until they drop","To attract insects to the plant flowers","To take water in from the soil surface"],"a":"To hold the plant up and carry water","hint":"The stem is like a highway for water transport.","exp":"The <b>stem</b> holds the plant upright and works as a transport highway, carrying water and minerals from the roots up to the leaves. Roots take in water, flowers attract insects and fruits hold the seeds."},{"id":"g3ssee-nat-031","q":"Which type of soil drains water very quickly?","opts":["Humus-rich soil","Sandy soil","Clay soil","Loamy soil"],"a":"Sandy soil","hint":"Think of what happens when water touches sand on a beach.","exp":"<b>Sandy soil</b> has large particles with big spaces between them, so water drains through it very quickly."},{"id":"g3ssee-nat-001","q":"Which part of a plant takes in water and minerals from the soil?","opts":["Leaves","Fruit","Flowers","Roots"],"a":"Roots","hint":"This part is underground.","exp":"<b>Roots</b> are found underground. They anchor the plant in the soil and absorb water and minerals needed for growth."},{"id":"g3ssee-nat-005","q":"Which part of a plant contains seeds?","opts":["Fruit","Leaf","Stem","Root"],"a":"Fruit","hint":"Think about what is inside a mango or papaya.","exp":"The <b>fruit</b> of a plant contains seeds. When the fruit is eaten or falls to the ground, seeds can grow into new plants."},{"id":"g3ssee-nat-041","q":"Which layer of soil contains most of the nutrients and living organisms?","opts":["Sand layer","Subsoil","Bedrock","Topsoil"],"a":"Topsoil","hint":"This is the top layer — the dark, rich one.","exp":"The <b>topsoil</b> is the uppermost layer of soil. It contains the most humus, nutrients and living organisms, which is why it is the most important layer for plant growth."}]}}};
